WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.260
The Avengers: Endgame team is still happy
they put the film's ladies in formation.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.940
In Slashfilm's definitive oral history of
the movie's enormous, climactic battle sequence,

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:13.780
Endgame co-writers Stephen McFeely and Christopher
Markus, editor Jeff Ford, and executive producer

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.920
Trinh Tran defended the decision to feature
all of the women of the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.900
in one shot — a moment that sparked controversy
among some fans.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.220
Female superheroes have been an integral part
of the MCU for years.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:30.240
However, the headlining superheroes throughout
the MCU's films have all been men, with Captain

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.740
Marvel as the only current exception.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.460
But with Captain Marvel confirmed as the MCU's
most powerful hero ...

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.080
… and with Black Widow's standalone prequel
movie forthcoming, this year was time for

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.740
these Marvel-ous women to finally get their
due, and the writers wanted to pay homage

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:46.400
to all of the incredible females in the MCU
during Endgame.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.520
Tran said the moment was nothing short of
magical, especially thanks to the on-set audience:

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:56.100
For his part, Ford recalled that the filming
process was awe-inspiring and so rewarding.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:00.320
He noted that the sequence was "one of the
most amazing things [he'd] ever seen" and

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.940
the work that went into it was nothing short
of impressive, adding,

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.720
"They brought so much attitude to that thing
that was just right on.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.680
We shot that shot about four or five times.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.220
It was all we could do, because it was crazy
-- it was really hard to do, because it involved

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.060
the camera moving on this small stage to get
enough running time.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.340
It wasn't a huge stage, but they ran a lot,
so we had to run them on this thing and the

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.100
rigging was really difficult to pull off."

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.940
When the Endgame team got the take they were
after, the cast and crew erupted in celebration.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.220
As Ford tells it,

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.600
"We got through it, and there was a big cheer,
and everybody was super happy, and all of

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.680
the women on the set got together and took
a group photo.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.980
I thought that was just the coolest thing
ever.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:38.360
I had chills that moment."

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:42.779
Those remarks come after Markus and McFeely
opened up to the New York Times in May 2019

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:43.460
about the scene.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.720
McFeely noted that the team "like[d] it too
much" to get rid of it, even with the risk

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:52.100
of it coming across as fan-service-y, while
Markus said that "part of the fun of the Avengers

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.000
movies has always been team-ups," and the
"badass women" deserved that kind of moment.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.160
Endgame co-director Anthony Russo also said
of the scene,

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:03.460
"Looking back on the entire road that the
MCU has traveled, it just struck us how many

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.819
amazing female characters have entered the
[franchise].

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:11.180
I think it was really, for us, a moment of
celebration and acknowledgment of the intensity

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:11.720
and empowerment in that."

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:17.391
As amazing as the Endgame team felt the all-female
scene was, the question still stood: Was it

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.960
a pandering effort to prove that the MCU is
female-friendly?

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.610
Ford admitted that he had his doubts about
the sequence early on, and Markus chimed in

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.580
to share a hilarious moment that happened
during a test screening, which ultimately

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.120
confirmed for him that the girl-power scene
needed to stay in Endgame.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.220
Markus said,

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.060
"Literally, one of the comments at a test
screening was, 'It's pandering.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:36.600
Don't you dare cut it."

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.640
McFeely's overall perspective on the scene's
importance was the most resonant:

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.940
"Emotional logic wins every time.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.820
So I recognize that there are some people
who are like, 'it's pretty convenient that

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.920
all of the ladies are all in one spot,' but
the eight-year-old girl next to me loses her

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.560
mind and wears a T-shirt, and I'm good with
it."

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:58.260
In the aftermath of Endgame's release, many
audience members felt that one brief moment

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.540
just wasn't enough service to the amazing
women of Marvel — especially since nobody

00:03:01.549 --> 00:03:05.859
speaks in the scene, instead quickly dispersing
to fight, and it doesn't really do anything

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.180
for the film's overall narrative.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.560
No matter what some might think about the
scene, it was certainly an encouraging effort

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:11.780
from the MCU.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.960
With plenty of female powerhouses coming up
in future films, the House of Heroes is sure

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.800
to make even more strides toward inclusivity
and representation.

